                        </svg>
                    </figure>
                    <p class="guide-caption">Solid arrows start at the side that opens the connection. Only the agent needs no inbound port on the server.</p>

                    <h3 id="agent" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="kind-dot" style={`--kind: ${kinds.agent.color}`}></span>Agent: the server pushes its data out</h3>
                    <p>
                        An agent is a program that runs on each server, reads the kernel's counters in <code>/proc</code> and the log files, and sends them to a backend over HTTPS. Because the server opens the connection, it works from behind NAT, from a private subnet, or from a customer site you can't reach. It also sees what nothing outside can: usage per mount, per-process counts, and the log line where the kernel killed a process.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The cost is software on every server that you install, configure and upgrade. Push has one blind spot: a server that dies sends nothing, and nothing looks like a quiet day unless an alert watches for it. The <a href="#heartbeat">heartbeat rule</a> covers that.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="agentless" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="kind-dot" style={`--kind: ${kinds.poller.color}`}></span>Agentless: a poller reaches in</h3>
                    <p>
                        Agentless monitoring runs on one central machine, the <strong>poller</strong>, which logs in or queries each server on a schedule. Nothing extra runs on the server, but every server has to accept a connection from the poller, and the poller holds credentials for all of them. An <strong>outside check</strong> is a lighter case: it only requests a public URL, so it needs no credentials and no extra port.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Method</th><th>Connection opened by</th><th>Open on the server</th><th>Fits</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {methods.map((row) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td class="guide-table__key">{row.method}</td>
                                        <td><span class="kind-badge" style={`--kind: ${kinds[row.kind].color}`}>{row.opens}</span></td>
                                        <td>{row.port}</td>
                                        <td>{row.fits}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        For servers you run, use an agent and add outside checks for the services people reach. Keep polling for devices that can't run an agent. The OpenTelemetry Collector covers each of these: <code>host_metrics</code> and <code>file_log</code> on the server, <code>snmp</code> and <code>ssh_check</code> for polling, and <code>http_check</code> for outside checks.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">Remote server monitoring tools</h3>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>License and price</th><th>Pick it if</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
