                                <tr><td class="guide-table__key"><a href="https://uptimerobot.com/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UptimeRobot</a></td><td><span class="kind-badge" style={`--kind: ${kinds.probe.color}`}>Outside check</span> as a hosted service</td><td>Free for 50 monitors at 5-minute intervals; paid plans check more often</td><td>You only need to know the site answers</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="guide-table__key"><a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/collector/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector</a></td><td><span class="kind-badge" style={`--kind: ${kinds.agent.color}`}>Agent</span>, poller and outside checks in one binary</td><td>Open source (Apache 2.0); sends to any OTLP backend</td><td>You want metrics and logs in an open format you can send anywhere</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="guide-caption">Prices from each vendor's pricing page, last verified October 31, 2026. This guide uses the Collector and sends to Maple.</p>

                    <h3 id="security" class="scroll-mt-24">Security follows the direction</h3>
                    <ul class="guide-list">
                        <li>With an agent, the server's firewall can deny all inbound monitoring traffic. Keep SSH for administration behind a VPN or a bastion host.</li>
                        <li>Send to an <code>https://</code> endpoint and leave certificate verification on. Don't set <code>insecure_skip_verify</code> on an exporter that crosses the internet.</li>
                        <li>A Maple ingest key can send telemetry and read nothing. Someone who copies it off a compromised server can send data to your organization and nothing more. Rotate it under <strong>Settings → Ingestion</strong>, which stops the old key at once, so roll out the new one first. See <a href="/docs/reference/authentication#ingest-keys">Authentication</a>.</li>
                        <li>SNMP v1 and v2c send the community string in plain text, and syslog over UDP is unencrypted. Use SNMP v3, or keep both inside a VPN.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section id="signals" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Signals</p>
                    <h2>Signals to watch on a remote server</h2>
                    <p>
                        Each row names the OpenTelemetry metric the setup below collects and a first alert condition. Replay a week of data before you page anyone on a threshold.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Signal</th><th>From</th><th>Collected as</th><th>Starting alert</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {signals.map((row) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td class="guide-table__key">{row.signal}</td>
                                        <td><span class="kind-badge" style={`--kind: ${kinds[row.kind].color}`}>{kinds[row.kind].label}</span></td>
                                        <td><code>{row.metric}</code></td>
                                        <td>{row.alert}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="disk" class="scroll-mt-24">Disk space and inodes</h3>
                    <p>
                        Disk is the signal most likely to take a remote server down while CPU and memory look normal. A log directory fills <code>/var</code>, the database can't write, and the service fails. Alert on every writable mount, including data volumes. Inodes run out too: a mount with free bytes and no free inodes rejects new files, and <code>df -i</code> shows it.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="ssh" class="scroll-mt-24">Check a remote server by hand over SSH</h3>
                    <p>
                        When an alert fires, a minute in a shell answers what a chart can't. These commands ship with most distributions:
                    </p>
                    <LanguageTabs tabs={sshTabs.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label }))} label="Resource" group="maple-guide-remote-resource">
                        {sshTabs.map((t) => (
                            <LanguageTab id={t.id}>
                                <pre><code set:html={highlight(t.code, "shell")} /></pre>
                                <p class="tab-note" set:html={t.read} />
                            </LanguageTab>
                        ))}
                    </LanguageTabs>
                    <p>For a quick pass over a few servers during an incident:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(sshLoop, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        A loop like this keeps no history and runs only while you watch, so it can't replace the setup below.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · Hands-on</p>
                    <h2>Set up remote server monitoring with the OpenTelemetry Collector</h2>
                    <p>
                        The <strong>OpenTelemetry Collector</strong> is an open-source program that receives, processes and exports telemetry. Its Contrib distribution, <code>otelcol-contrib</code>, includes every receiver this guide uses. It runs in two roles here: as the <span class="kind-text" style={`--kind: ${kinds.agent.color}`}>agent</span> on each Debian, Ubuntu or RHEL-family server, and as the <span class="kind-text" style={`--kind: ${kinds.probe.color}`}>checker</span> on one small machine in a different network. Both send OTLP to Maple. Every config here was run on release 0.163.1.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">1. Install the Collector on each server</h3>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(installCommands, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Use the <code>arm64</code> package on ARM servers, and the current version from the <a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-releases/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">releases page</a>. The package creates an unprivileged <code>otelcol-contrib</code> user, a systemd unit that runs as that user, and <code>/etc/otelcol-contrib/</code> with <code>config.yaml</code> and an environment file, <code>otelcol-contrib.conf</code>, which the unit reads at start.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="agent-config" class="scroll-mt-24">2. Configure the agent</h3>
                    <p>Replace <code>/etc/otelcol-contrib/config.yaml</code> with:</p>
                    <div class="code-file" style={`--kind: ${kinds.agent.color}`}>
                        <p class="code-file__name">config.yaml <span>on each server</span></p>
                        <pre><code set:html={highlight(agentConfig, "yaml")} /></pre>
                    </div>
                    <ul class="guide-list">
                        <li><code>host_metrics</code> reads CPU, load, memory, filesystems, network and process counts every 30 seconds. The three <code>*.utilization</code> metrics are off by default and are the ones the Maple Hosts page reads.</li>
                        <li><code>file_log</code> tails the system logs from the end, so a restart doesn't resend old lines. They arrive unparsed under the service <code>system-logs</code>. RHEL-family systems use <code>/var/log/messages</code> and <code>/var/log/secure</code>, and Debian 12 writes only to the journal unless <code>rsyslog</code> is installed, so list the files that exist.</li>
                        <li><code>resource_detection</code> sets <code>host.name</code>, <code>os.type</code> and <code>host.arch</code>. Its <code>env</code> detector reads <code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code>, so per-server values go in the environment file and <code>config.yaml</code> stays the same on every server.</li>
                        <li>EU organizations use <code>https://ingest.eu.maple.dev</code>.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Older Collector releases</p>
                        <p>
                            Releases before the component rename call these <code>hostmetrics</code>, <code>filelog</code>, <code>resourcedetection</code>, <code>otlphttp</code>, <code>httpcheck</code> and <code>sshcheck</code>. Release 0.163.1 still accepts the old names and logs a deprecation warning for each.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="start" class="scroll-mt-24">3. Add the key and start the service</h3>
                    <p>
                        Put the private ingest key (<code>maple_sk_…</code>) from <strong>Settings → Ingestion</strong> in <code>/etc/otelcol-contrib/otelcol-contrib.conf</code>:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(envFile, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Then make that file readable by root only, let the service user read <code>/var/log</code> (on Debian and Ubuntu the log files belong to the <code>adm</code> group), and start the service:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(serviceCommands, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        systemd reads the environment file as root before it drops to the service user, so the key never sits in <code>config.yaml</code>. A <code>401</code> in the journal means the key is wrong. On RHEL-family systems the log files are readable by root only and the <code>adm</code> step doesn't apply: give <code>otelcol-contrib</code> read access with an ACL, or leave <code>file_log</code> out.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="checker" class="scroll-mt-24">4. Check the servers from outside</h3>
                    <p>
                        Install the same package on one small VM in a different network or provider, so it keeps checking when a server's network fails. Its <code>http_check</code> receiver requests each URL every minute. An <code>ssh_check</code> receiver logs in to a server with no web endpoint and reports whether the login worked; add one per server, named <code>ssh_check/&lt;name&gt;</code>.
                    </p>
                    <div class="code-file" style={`--kind: ${kinds.probe.color}`}>
                        <p class="code-file__name">config.yaml <span>on the checker</span></p>
                        <pre><code set:html={highlight(checkerConfig, "yaml")} /></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Create a <code>monitor</code> user with a key on <code>db-01</code>, copy the private key to the checker with owner <code>otelcol-contrib</code> and mode <code>0600</code>, and record the server's host keys:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(knownHosts, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>ssh-keyscan</code> records every key type the server offers. With only one of them in <code>known_hosts</code>, the check can fail with <code>knownhosts: key mismatch</code>. The receivers report <code>httpcheck.status</code> per status class, <code>httpcheck.duration</code>, <code>httpcheck.tls.cert_remaining</code> in seconds, and <code>sshcheck.status</code> (1 or 0) per <code>ssh.endpoint</code>. The <a href="/docs/alerting/uptime-monitoring#alert-when-an-endpoint-stops-answering">uptime monitoring docs</a> have the alert rules for these metrics, and <a href="/guides/uptime-monitoring">Uptime monitoring with traces and HTTP checks</a> covers which failures a check catches.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="fleet" class="scroll-mt-24">Many servers, one config</h3>
                    <p>
                        Maple lists each distinct <code>host.name</code> as its own host. A VM cloned from an image that kept its hostname merges two servers into one row, and a rename splits one into two. Pin the name in the environment file, where the <code>env</code> detector reads it before the <code>system</code> detector looks at the OS. With Ansible, template <code>otelcol-contrib.conf</code>:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(envTemplate, "shell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Ship <code>config.yaml</code> unchanged, deploy the environment file with mode <code>0600</code>, restart the service when either changes, and pin the package version in the same playbook so an upgrade is one change you can roll back.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · In Maple</p>
                    <h2>Remote servers on the Maple Hosts page</h2>
                    <p>
                        Open <strong>Infrastructure → Hosts</strong>. A server appears within about a minute of its agent starting. The list shows each host's status, CPU, memory and disk usage, 15-minute load and last report, and you can filter it by status to find the servers that went quiet:
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table class="status-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Status</th><th>Last report</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td class="guide-table__key" data-status="active">Active</td><td>Less than 1 minute ago</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="guide-table__key" data-status="idle">Idle</td><td>Between 1 and 5 minutes ago</td></tr>
